{"task_id": "HumanEval/96", "solution": "def count_up_to(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds all prime numbers less than a given non-negative integer n.\n    It efficiently implements the Sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n <= 2:\n        return []\n\n    # Helper function for Sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm.\n    def _sieve_of_eratosthenes_internal(limit: int) -> list[bool]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Generates a boolean list indicating primality up to 'limit' (inclusive).\n        True signifies a prime number at that index, False otherwise.\n        \"\"\"\n        is_prime = [True] * (limit + 1)\n        is_prime[0] = is_prime[1] = False # 0 and 1 are explicitly not prime.\n\n        # Iterate from p = 2 up to sqrt(limit) to mark multiples.\n        for p in range(2, int(limit**0.5) + 1):\n            if is_prime[p]:\n                # Mark all multiples of p as not prime, starting from p*p.\n                for multiple in range(p * p, limit + 1, p):\n                    is_prime[multiple] = False\n        return is_prime\n\n    # Generate the primality list for numbers up to n-1.\n    is_prime_list = _sieve_of_eratosthenes_internal(n - 1)\n\n    # Collect all numbers marked as prime that are less than n.\n    primes = []\n    for num in range(2, n):\n        if is_prime_list[num]:\n            primes.append(num)\n            \n    return primes"}
